WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.730
Our new lunch model found the cheapest

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.750
plate that meets every need: forty-one

3
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:06.090
coins.

4
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:07.970
The cooks laughed.

5
00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.050
Two handfuls of groundnuts and half a cup

6
00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.750
of greens, every day, for every child?

7
00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.240
Today: when the model is wrong, and how

8
00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.320
to find out before the children do.

9
00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.430
A model obeys only the rules it is given.

10
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.100
Nobody told this one about variety, taste,

11
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.000
allergies or how long rice takes to cook.

12
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.860
So it did exactly what it was asked,

13
00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.840
and the answer is still wrong.

14
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.200
Find the missing rules by showing the

15
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.160
answer to the people who live with it.

16
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.400
The cooks, the pupils, a parent

17
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.780
with an allergic child: each spots

18
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.680
something the model can’t.

19
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.970
Each objection becomes a new

20
00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.130
limit or a new goal.

21
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:58.840
The cooks say: no more than one

22
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.420
handful of groundnuts.

23
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.560
Solve again, and lunch costs about

24
00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.340
forty-three coins, two more than before.

25
00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:09.530
And look: the model now serves a

26
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.890
spoonful of beans, because nobody

27
00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.430
said portions come in halves.

28
00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:16.290
Models can also be wrong

29
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.550
because their numbers are.

30
00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:20.530
A price goes up at the market, a

31
00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.710
road floods, a teacher leaves.

32
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.940
Before you trust a plan, change the

33
00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.540
numbers you are least sure of, and see

34
00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.280
whether the plan changes a lot.

35
00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.790
Flood one bridge, and the shortest way

36
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.610
to school goes from thirteen kilometres

37
00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.070
to fourteen, by a new route.

38
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.740
Raise the price of tomatoes, and

39
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.680
the best garden plan jumps from

40
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.420
three tomato beds to six.

41
00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.330
A plan that jumps when one number

42
00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.150
moves needs a second look.

43
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:50.990
Quick check!

44
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.120
The model’s cheapest lunch has no

45
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.140
beans and no eggs at all.

46
00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.110
What’s the most likely reason?

47
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.510
The model has no rule about

48
00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.830
variety, so it skips them.

49
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.960
The solver did its job; the

50
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.300
model left something out.

51
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.800
Mistakes in models are usually

52
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.120
missing rules, not wrong sums.

53
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.860
So write the model’s assumptions down,

54
00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.280
in plain words, beside the answer.

55
00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.930
Which needs it meets, which prices it

56
00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:28.510
used and when, and what it ignores.

57
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:32.550
Then anyone, a cook or a parent, can

58
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.150
check it and say what’s missing.

59
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.340
In the app, find the cheapest lunch, then

60
00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.700
allow up to four portions of a food.

61
00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.710
The cheapest plate becomes

62
00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:44.390
almost all groundnuts.

63
00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.520
Then take foods out of the

64
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.140
market, and see what happens.

65
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:49.960
Your turn.

66
00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:52.910
The pupils say the cheapest plan is

67
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:55.410
fine, but the cooks say it can’t be

68
00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:56.390
cooked in time for lunch.

69
00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.470
Who is right about the model?

70
00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:01.770
The cooks: cooking time is a missing

71
00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.150
limit, so the plan can’t really happen.

72
00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.530
Remember: a model obeys only its

73
00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.970
rules, so test answers with the

74
00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:11.010
people who live with them.

75
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.920
Change the shaky numbers and see

76
00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.080
whether the plan jumps.

77
00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:17.170
And write the assumptions

78
00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:18.210
beside the answer.

79
00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.030
Try it in class tomorrow: show pupils a

80
00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.470
plan a computer could make, such as the

81
00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.810
cheapest lunch, and ask them to break it.

82
00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.210
Every objection becomes a

83
00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:30.210
rule on the board.

84
00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:34.170
Then ask: which rules did the first model

85
00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.070
miss, and who would have noticed?
